## Summary

- close the fixed-block part of the Nie `B₂` to Vandaele `{X,CX,CCX}` fidelity gap with executable NCT circuits;
- certify an exact four-`CCX` `C³X` gadget using one arbitrary dirty bit;
- certify an exact ten-`CCX` `C⁴X` gadget, again restoring the arbitrary dirty bit;
- prove exact gate count and sequential depth from the same proof-bearing schedules;
- exhaustively kernel-check all 32 and 64 computational-basis states via finite `native_decide`.

## Source-fidelity significance

Nie's source theorem is stated over `B₂`, while Vandaele Lemma 1 advertises `{CCX,CX,X}`. These fixed gadgets make the bounded recursive blocks explicit instead of silently importing a stronger gate-set claim.

## Verification

Dedicated workflow `Vandaele borrowed NCT gadgets` passes on Lean 4.29.1 at commit `72dddfca67002cff8619bcb687b57ce5ab0cf212` (run `34144193095`).

## Harness boundary

This PR certifies only the fixed `k=3,4` NCT leaves. It does not yet claim the uniform recursive family or the final `O(k)` / `O(log k)` theorem.

## Summary

- define exact reverse schedules for reversible X/CX/CCX programs;
- prove reverse execution restores the complete computational-basis state;
- package a child construction as `prepare`, persistent `commit`, and delayed `cleanup`;
- expose the Nie Step-2 `forwardHalf` separately from Step-4 cleanup;
- prove `forwardHalf.depth + cleanup.depth = full.depth` and the analogous gate-count identity.

## Why this matters for Nie/Vandaele

Nie Figure 3 recursively uses the first half of child constructions before the parent action and postpones cleanup until later. Treating Step 2 and Step 4 as two complete recursive calls would give the wrong recurrence. This certificate keeps the intermediate state and delayed uncomputation explicit, which is the structure needed for the `D(n)=D(n/2)+O(1)` proof.

## Verification

Dedicated workflow `Reversible compute action uncompute` passes on Lean 4.29.1 at commit `6966b28b5ef4eebf571f28b3c65cfda0e3614952`.

## Harness boundary

This PR proves the reusable split/inverse machinery only. It does not yet instantiate Nie Figure 3 or claim the uniform Vandaele Lemma 1 family.

## Summary

- define layer- and schedule-level cross-wire-disjointness certificates;
- merge corresponding valid layers into one proof-bearing parallel schedule;
- prove merged schedule validity from internal validity plus cross-disjointness;
- prove exact logical gate-count additivity;
- prove certified depth is exactly the maximum of the two input depths.

## Why this matters for Vandaele/Nie

The logarithmic-depth recursion needs two child computations to share time slices. This PR makes that resource claim proof-bearing: gate count and depth are both read from the same merged schedule, rather than from detached numeric annotations.

## Verification

Dedicated workflow `Reversible parallel schedule` passes on Lean 4.29.1 at commit `49ac0f2a7ebfbe92dcbc824ceacb6e02bf66968c`.

## Harness boundary

This PR proves schedule validity and resource accounting only. Semantic preservation of the interleaved schedule is intentionally separated and depends on the disjoint-gate commutation theorem in sibling PR #72.

## Summary

- put `X`, `CX`, and `CCX` into one activation-plus-target-flip semantic normal form;
- prove flipping an untouched wire preserves another gate's activation predicate;
- derive target separation from `ReversibleGate.WireDisjoint`;
- prove bit flips on distinct targets commute;
- conclude that any two wire-disjoint reversible gates commute on every computational-basis state.

## Why this matters for Vandaele/Nie

The logarithmic-depth recursion needs to merge two child computations into common time slices. The schedule-level depth theorem is only semantically sound once cross-disjoint gates are known to commute. This PR supplies that reusable gate-level semantic foundation; lifting it to layers/programs is a separate downstream frontier.

## Verification

Dedicated workflow `Reversible disjoint commutation` passes on Lean 4.29.1.

## Harness boundary

This PR does not yet claim that a whole merged schedule equals a sequential child ordering, nor does it claim the full Vandaele Lemma 1 resource theorem.

## Summary

- add a generic bridge from the flat proof-bearing `LemmaOneFlatSpec` to the source Definition-2.1 `multiControlledXEquiv` semantics;
- implement the exact `k = 0, 1, 2` inhabitants with native `X`, `CX`, and `CCX` reversible gates;
- prove controls are preserved, target behavior is exact, and the dirty workspace is restored;
- derive gate count and parallel depth from the same `ScheduledReversibleProgram` objects;
- repair the existing `ReversibleSchedule` and `VandaeleLemma1ProgramFamily` proof scripts for Lean 4.29 so the executable refinement layer can compile.

## Harness boundary

This closes the primitive leaves of the Vandaele/Nie recursion. It does **not** extrapolate these leaves into the generic `Theta(k)` / `Theta(log k)` family. The uniform recursive construction remains a separate proof frontier.

## Verification

Dedicated workflow `Vandaele Lemma 1 primitive base cases` passes on Lean 4.29.1.

## Summary

- formalize injectively selected control blocks and their X-normalization action;
- encode the source-critical distinction between globally clean ancillas and conditionally-clean input wires;
- prove that a flag certifying selected controls were all one makes those wires zero only on the `flag = true` branch;
- prove normalization is involutive, so the full control register is restored by cleanup.

## Harness boundary

This is a semantic invariant used by the Nie/Vandaele recursion. It deliberately does **not** claim a full recursive `{X,CX,CCX}` implementation or the `Theta(k)` / `Theta(log k)` resource theorem. Those require proof-bearing recursive schedules and disjoint parallel composition.

## Verification

Dedicated workflow `Vandaele Lemma 1 conditionally-clean invariant` passes on Lean 4.29.1.

## Summary

- specialize the reusable involutory clean-to-dirty protocol to Vandaele Definition 2.1 `C^k X` semantics;
- prove exact target action for every `k` and every incoming dirty bit;
- prove the dirty bit is restored and the external controls/target refine `multiControlledXEquiv`;
- keep protocol accounting separate from the still-open `{X,CX,CCX}` recursive gate-schedule and asymptotic resource proof.

## Harness boundary

This is a semantic worker branch stacked on the source-facing Lemma 1 contract. It does **not** claim the Nie/Vandaele `Theta(k)` gate or `Theta(log k)` depth construction. Those remain downstream executable obligations.

## Verification

Dedicated workflow `Vandaele Lemma 1 dirty semantic bridge` passes on Lean 4.29.1.

Conflict-free proof-admission PR cut directly from the current `codex/sp-papers-comparator-proof-cost` head.

This PR intentionally contains only the semantic closure layer above the already-verified proof branch:
- reusable conditional bit-toggle algebra;
- `C_L ⟂ X'` selected-register noninterference;
- enhanced three-stage Algorithm-2 semantics and recursive readback;
- complete semantic aggregate including all six source-target cases;
- final strong-induction theorem refining Definition 6 / Equation (7);
- cached aggregate/final CI workflows.

It is an admission microscope, not a direct `main` integration PR. Once green, the proof-bearing theorem files can be promoted into the proof branch and downstream Vandaele/comparator/incrementer formalization can continue.

Temporary compiler-admission PR for the isolated Remaud–Vandaele Algorithm 2 / Equation-(7) proof line.

Why this PR exists:
- the previous admission PR shared `codex/sp-papers-comparator-proof-cost` with unrelated Vandaele/Nie formalization work, and that base ref was later moved backwards;
- the Algorithm-2 proof work is therefore pinned to its own stable base `codex/vandaele-alpha-equation7-proof`;
- this PR must remain draft and must not be merged directly into `main`.

Current admission scope:
- exact physical `X'` construction and exact `alpha'` rank certificate;
- canonical recursive child plan;
- proof-bearing Algorithm-2 outer walls and recursive schedule;
- exact source gate-count/depth recurrences;
- `m=0,1` semantic base cases;
- target support / stage noninterference;
- ordinary, special-tail, final-target and non-alpha semantic geometry;
- local ordinary-pair cancellation algebra.

No semantic correctness assumption is admitted by the frontier marker: the marker is only an import target forcing Lean to compile the transitive proof DAG. After this DAG is green, the final strong-induction `algorithm_refines_equationSeven` theorem will be added as the next admission layer.
